# id  tag  kind  t_s  alt_km  L_si_W  flag  note
1 - first_light 0.0030 100.000 1.9201e+05 info  L_si first exceeds 1e-6 of its peak. This is just a visibility threshold; the real value depends on your sensor floor and on where the simulation starts (zstart).
2 A ablation_onset 1.7970 83.449 3.6416e+06 stylized  Net heating flux first exceeds radiative loss at T_ablate = 2300 K, so ablation switches on (a kink in the slope). Real onset is gradual over a few km. BELOW this point the model's light is drag work only (no ablated material): treat light above ~83 km as low-confidence. Loosely like the end of Phase 1 / start of Phase 2 in [R22], but the model has no spin, volatile (Na/K) emission or melt physics.
3 Y first_yield 5.4930 49.620 9.2151e+08 physical  Loading rho v^2 reaches S0 = 0.33 MPa. The body starts to flatten (pancake): area, drag and heating all grow, so the light rises faster. Analogue of the onset of fragmentation (Phase 5 / H_f in [R22]).
4 F flare 5.5857 48.778 2.5632e+09 stylized  Light rises x19.3 in 0.26 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: yield gen 0 at 49.6 km (99% of m0); split gen 0 at 48.0 km (99% of m0). Dust groups supply 82% of the rise and 78% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
5 B first_breakup 5.6670 48.039 5.2566e+09 stylized  Spread factor reached f_split = 2: the body breaks into 4 children plus 20 % of its mass as dust. The dust has a large area-to-mass ratio, decelerates and ablates within a fraction of a second, which makes a sharp flare. The instantaneous release is a model simplification. Light grew x5.7 between first yield and this breakup (pancake spreading).
6 F flare 6.0010 45.045 2.7513e+10 stylized  Light rises x1.3 in 0.08 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: split gen 1 at 44.9 km (37% of m0); yield gen 1 at 44.5 km (19% of m0). Dust groups supply 96% of the rise and 63% of the light at the end of the flare (the model releases dust all at once, so a dust-dominated first flare is by construction). A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
7 b bump 6.3346 42.100 5.7756e+10 stylized  Small fluctuation, 7% above the local trend; coincides with a split (gen 2) at 41.9 km. The fine structure of this phase is set by the cascade rules, so its pattern is not predictive of a real event.
8 b bump 6.5248 40.480 6.3792e+10 stylized  Small fluctuation, 11% above the local trend; coincides with a split (gen 2) at 40.3 km. The fine structure of this phase is set by the cascade rules, so its pattern is not predictive of a real event.
9 P peak 6.5250 40.478 6.3827e+10 physical  Maximum light. It sits ~+1.7 km from the peak of energy deposition (38.8 km) because the luminous efficiency tau(v) falls as the fragments slow (mass-weighted speed here 15.9 km/s).
10 - drag_overtakes_ablation 6.8310 38.036 4.7655e+10 stylized  Drag work now exceeds the kinetic energy carried off by ablated mass. The ratio ablation/drag is about Lambda v^2/(2 Q Cd), so this happens when the speed falls below ~14 km/s (from the model's heating law).
11 D rolloff 7.1456 35.834 2.3480e+10 physical  Light has fallen to 1/e of the peak 0.62 s after it. Cause: the kinetic energy is being used up (speed 67% of v0, 43% of the mass still in flight) and tau(v) drops with speed. Analogue of Phase 7 (decline until dark flight) in [R22].
12 T tail 8.8798 29.101 6.3680e+07 low-confidence  Below 1e-3 of the peak the light is controlled by groups being retired when they slow below v_dark = 3.0 km/s and by the tau taper (2-4 km/s). Do not interpret structure below this level.
